# Line endings are decided here, not by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# Without this, git falls back to each developer's core.autocrlf. On Windows
# that is usually true, so every file written by anything that emits LF gets
# rewritten on the next `git add` — and the warning arrives twelve at a time,
# for files whose content did not change. Worse, it makes a diff between two
# machines show whole files as modified.
#
# eol=lf rather than eol=native on purpose. The estate builds on Windows, Linux
# and macOS, and the checked-in bytes should be the same everywhere; Rust,
# .NET, PowerShell 7 and every editor in use read LF without complaint.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never inspects or converts them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.pdb binary

# Windows-only tooling that genuinely wants CRLF. A .cmd or .bat run by
# cmd.exe is the one case where LF is not merely unconventional but wrong.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
